WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:04.720
BUSSEY: Welcome everybody.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:11.200
We're going to go a few minutes later than
planned, to about 9:20, and we'll have a good

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:16.340
opportunity for a robust question-and-answer
session.

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:21.880
So, please, be thinking of some robust questions
as we go along in our conversation this morning.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:24.869
Just a reminder that this is on the record.

00:00:24.869 --> 00:00:28.419
When you ask your questions later on, I'll
just be asking you to identify yourself.

00:00:28.419 --> 00:00:34.750
If you can put your ringers off on your cellphones,
just put it on stun, you know, whatever setting

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:38.040
you have, that would be very helpful to us.

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:41.680
And since we have a lot to talk about, let's
jump right into it.

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:44.920
Secretary Lew, thank you very much for joining
us.

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:48.090
This feels like an historic week.

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:53.030
It's not quite like a moon landing, but we
appear to be very close to having a budget,

00:00:53.030 --> 00:00:57.030
which is -- seems an unusual thing for the
United States of America.

00:00:57.030 --> 00:01:03.150
It's not without a stopgap or so, but it looks
like this is going to move fairly quickly

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:04.150
now.

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:09.870
I wonder if you can talk about how this might
affect business confidence, consumer confidence,

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:16.630
going into the 2014 calendar economic year,
how it affects your outlook on growth in the

00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:17.630
U.S.?

00:01:17.630 --> 00:01:22.610
But also do so in the context, if you would,
of this -- the strange number we got on jobs

00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:26.950
in December, which seemed like it was out
of the ordinary from the trend line, which

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:32.320
had been quite positive before, a weak jobs
number in December, if that was an anomaly

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:34.550
or if there was something that worries you
in it.

00:01:34.550 --> 00:01:36.159
LEW: Well, John, it's good to be here.

00:01:36.159 --> 00:01:37.890
Thanks so much for doing this.

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:41.409
I think we're starting the year off strong.

00:01:41.409 --> 00:01:47.180
We're starting the year off with economic
tailwinds, not headwinds, and I'll get back

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:50.920
to the specific number you asked about.

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:57.140
And I think we're starting out with Congress
following through on the end of last year,

00:01:57.140 --> 00:02:05.180
which was trying to get back to some kind
of normalcy and away from the kind of brinksmanship

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:13.080
and uncertainty that was causing a great deal
of impact to confidence in the United States

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:14.310
and internationally.

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:19.380
Obviously, we're, you know, not out of the
woods completely.

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:22.790
We still have a lot more to do to keep our
economy growing.

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:30.840
But if you look at the arc of the last half-year,
the trend of economic statistics -- job statistics,

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:33.700
confidence -- have been strong.

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:35.319
It's been strong across sectors.

00:02:35.319 --> 00:02:39.959
And there's potential for more growth in a
number of key areas.

00:02:39.959 --> 00:02:44.300
I do think that the mood in Washington makes
a difference, in two different ways.

00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:46.319
One, the policy actually matters.

00:02:46.319 --> 00:02:50.770
The fact is that the budget agreement, which
is now being implemented through these appropriation

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:54.340
bills, takes away some of the fiscal drag
from the economy.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:56.270
That actually matters.

00:02:56.270 --> 00:02:57.270
Macroeconomics matter.

00:02:57.270 --> 00:03:03.140
And going from policies that push back on
economic growth to creating a little bit more

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:05.040
economic energy help.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:13.520
But the question of business-as-usual versus
dysfunction is extremely significant.

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:17.600
Just a few months ago, we had a government
shutdown, we were in the midst of a crisis

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:23.569
over the borrowing authority of the United
States, and it was very undermining of confidence.

00:03:23.569 --> 00:03:28.810
Now, the reason I say we're not out of the
woods completely is that as long as we still

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:33.709
have so many Americans looking for work, our
job is to focus on more growth and more jobs,

00:03:33.709 --> 00:03:36.739
and we still have some deadlines ahead of
us here in Washington.

00:03:36.739 --> 00:03:39.521
We have, you know, a deadline looming in February.

00:03:39.521 --> 00:03:42.042
February 7th our borrowing authority runs
out again.

00:03:42.042 --> 00:03:46.430
At that point, Congress has to act to get
the borrowing authority.

00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:48.150
BUSSEY: We'll get to -- we'll get -- we'll
come around to that.

00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:50.771
Tell us a little bit about December, though,
about what happened with the jobs number.

00:03:50.771 --> 00:03:57.330
LEW: So it -- I've spent most of the last
three-and-a-half decades advising policymakers

00:03:57.330 --> 00:04:03.120
not to react to each month-to-month number
too strongly, to look at the overall pattern.

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:08.659
We will only know in several months exactly
what that number means.

00:04:08.659 --> 00:04:12.990
I've read an enormous range of views as to
what the number means.

00:04:12.990 --> 00:04:18.629
I think you have to look at the kind of trend
that has been clear for several months, and

00:04:18.629 --> 00:04:22.889
I don't think that one ought to look at a
number that deviates from that as being in

00:04:22.889 --> 00:04:27.830
and of itself proof that -- that there's been
a fundamental change.

00:04:27.830 --> 00:04:34.590
Economies don't turn on a dime, so all of
the evidence that there was strength in the

00:04:34.590 --> 00:04:35.590
economy is still there.

00:04:35.590 --> 00:04:39.000
And, you know, our job is to make sure we
promote more growth.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:41.350
Our job is to make sure we promote more investment.

00:04:41.350 --> 00:04:43.530
There are things we can do.

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:47.710
You know, there's a debate in Washington now
about whether or not to extend unemployment

00:04:47.710 --> 00:04:48.750
benefits.

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:52.080
You can look at that in two different ways,
and both are right.

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:56.880
One, it's a question of just simple fairness,
people looking for work, you know, out of

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:01.530
work because of the deep recession that we
took a long time to come out of, they need

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:02.690
the help.

00:05:02.690 --> 00:05:06.590
You can also look at it as a macroeconomic
question.

00:05:06.590 --> 00:05:10.840
All those people who are looking for work
are also putting food on the table and taking

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:14.840
care of the necessities of life, and every
penny that they get, they spend.

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:17.100
And that's economic activity.

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:23.199
So I hope that we get back to the discussion
of how to extend unemployment benefits.

00:05:23.199 --> 00:05:28.860
We can get in the discussion to other questions
of things that we can do, but, you know, the

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:36.380
more we do consistent with the spirit of the
budget agreement that takes step after step

00:05:36.380 --> 00:05:38.511
to make progress, the better the economy's
going to be.

00:05:38.511 --> 00:05:42.560
You look at the budget agreement, there are
actually some things in there that, as people

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:46.740
focus on them, you know, there's funding for
manufacturing centers.

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:49.759
There's funding for early childhood education.

00:05:49.759 --> 00:05:52.551
There are things in there that are going to
be good for today and for the future of the

00:05:52.551 --> 00:05:56.590
American economy, and we can continue to take
steps along that path.

00:05:56.590 --> 00:05:59.340
What the infrastructure needs in this country,
it would help in the short term, it would

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:00.340
help in the long term.

00:06:00.340 --> 00:06:04.539
BUSSEY: So there have been a lot of good numbers
in the last several months, a pretty healthy

00:06:04.539 --> 00:06:05.889
trajectory.

00:06:05.889 --> 00:06:09.850
Manufacturing growth, export growth, November
numbers were really good.

00:06:09.850 --> 00:06:16.150
One number in December that was consistent
with previous months is the erosion of -- of

00:06:16.150 --> 00:06:22.139
the denominator in the unemployment calculation,
that is, people leaving the workforce, giving

00:06:22.139 --> 00:06:24.290
up looking for work.

00:06:24.290 --> 00:06:27.710
How does that affect your growth forecast
for 2014?

00:06:27.710 --> 00:06:33.020
Are you still on the -- is it still expected
to be in the 2 percent, you know, range for

00:06:33.020 --> 00:06:34.020
the calendar year?

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:37.900
LEW: Well, we'll obviously come out with our
new economic projections in a few weeks when

00:06:37.900 --> 00:06:40.910
-- when the budget comes out.

00:06:40.910 --> 00:06:44.880
I'm not going to kind of lift the curtain
early on them, except to say that we ended

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:45.880
the year...

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:46.880
BUSSEY: Just do that here.

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:47.880
I mean, we'll keep it just between us.

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:48.880
(LAUGHTER)

00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:54.030
LEW: We -- we ended the year with -- with
confidence that the economy was doing better,

00:06:54.030 --> 00:06:57.069
and we start the year with confidence that
it's continuing to do better.

00:06:57.069 --> 00:07:02.870
You know, the long-term unemployment numbers,
the labor force participation rate numbers,

00:07:02.870 --> 00:07:08.600
they're very serious issues that we spend
a lot of time poring over and asking what's

00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:13.210
causing it, what are the policy responses.

00:07:13.210 --> 00:07:18.910
There are a lot of, again, competing explanations,
some of them demographic, some of them cyclical,

00:07:18.910 --> 00:07:20.400
some of them structural...

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:21.400
BUSSEY: People getting older and leaving the
workforce normally.

00:07:21.400 --> 00:07:24.680
LEW: People are getting older, and there's
some normal transitions going on.

00:07:24.680 --> 00:07:29.729
It's been a long, slow recovery from a very
deep financially-led recession, so there's

00:07:29.729 --> 00:07:34.850
a delay in some of the employment growth.

00:07:34.850 --> 00:07:37.800
But it's that structural piece that we have
to worry about.

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:41.860
We really have to ask ourselves, if somebody
graduated from high school or college and

00:07:41.860 --> 00:07:47.090
didn't go to work and didn't get right into
the labor force, what can we do to make it

00:07:47.090 --> 00:07:50.259
easier for them to get started?

00:07:50.259 --> 00:07:54.560
What can we do to take away the skill gap
that may be preventing them from getting started?

00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:59.490
And, frankly, what can we do to work with
employers to take away some of stigma that

00:07:59.490 --> 00:08:02.630
is associated with not having worked for a
period of time?

00:08:02.630 --> 00:08:09.430
You know, if you graduated college or high
school when unemployment was, you know, close

00:08:09.430 --> 00:08:13.750
to 10 percent, it was really hard to get that
first job.

00:08:13.750 --> 00:08:18.250
And the fact that you're now delayed in getting
started is something we have to worry about,

00:08:18.250 --> 00:08:19.250
how do you catch up?

00:08:19.250 --> 00:08:23.130
And, you know, frankly, that's something that's
a question of public policy, but it's also

00:08:23.130 --> 00:08:27.680
a question of working with the private sector
to make sure that employers are thinking about,

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:29.870
how do we give those kids a chance?

00:08:29.870 --> 00:08:32.070
BUSSEY: So you mentioned February 7th.

00:08:32.070 --> 00:08:36.639
That's yet another potential stumbling block
for the economy, another point of uncertainty.

00:08:36.639 --> 00:08:40.139
It's the debt ceiling deadline.

00:08:40.139 --> 00:08:45.509
Having been through this drama a couple of
times now in the last couple of years, and

00:08:45.509 --> 00:08:50.019
seeing the effect on confidence, the economy,
the markets, wouldn't it be in the Treasury's

00:08:50.019 --> 00:08:53.640
interest to say, look, here's our battle plan.

00:08:53.640 --> 00:09:00.230
If this thing goes to the wire again, if it's
a cliffhanger, these are our priorities for

00:09:00.230 --> 00:09:05.730
spending, this is how we're going to martial
our funds, we'll buy enough time past the

00:09:05.730 --> 00:09:11.470
end of February, not just for a couple of
weeks, to see the economy through, don't worry,

00:09:11.470 --> 00:09:14.220
markets, don't worry, business investment,
don't worry, consumer?

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:19.929
LEW: Well, John, I don't think any treasury
secretary of either party has ever said, "Don't

00:09:19.929 --> 00:09:26.000
worry about what happens if the United States
loses its capacity to borrow," because it's

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:28.699
something to worry about.

00:09:28.699 --> 00:09:34.249
We -- if we ever get to the place where the
United States cannot keep all of its obligations,

00:09:34.249 --> 00:09:39.230
we are fundamentally hurting our -- our standing
as a country that has always honored all of

00:09:39.230 --> 00:09:40.579
its obligations.

00:09:40.579 --> 00:09:44.959
So I actually don't believe that there is
any plan that could give the kind of confidence

00:09:44.959 --> 00:09:46.200
that you're describing.

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:51.790
You know, people ask legalistic questions
about, what do you have the ability to do?

00:09:51.790 --> 00:09:56.240
And I'm asking the more fundamental question,
why would anyone want to hurt the U.S. economy

00:09:56.240 --> 00:10:02.850
and hurt the recipients of payments that they're
entitled to because of a self-inflicted wound,

00:10:02.850 --> 00:10:09.069
because of something that -- everyone knows
that the obligations are not made when the

00:10:09.069 --> 00:10:10.449
borrowing authority is raised.

00:10:10.449 --> 00:10:14.930
The obligations are being made when you vote
on appropriation bills, when you vote on tax

00:10:14.930 --> 00:10:16.149
bills.

00:10:16.149 --> 00:10:21.879
In the end, the borrowing authority merely
allows you to operate under the policy decisions

00:10:21.879 --> 00:10:22.879
you've made.

00:10:22.879 --> 00:10:23.879
Congress...

00:10:23.879 --> 00:10:24.879
BUSSEY: It's spending you've already done.

00:10:24.879 --> 00:10:27.589
LEW: Yeah, spending, the combination of policies.

00:10:27.589 --> 00:10:31.290
Now, I -- this is Congress's unique responsibility.

00:10:31.290 --> 00:10:32.559
Congress is going to have to act.

00:10:32.559 --> 00:10:40.209
It is not, I think, something that anyone
should want to repeat, the kind of, you know,

00:10:40.209 --> 00:10:49.170
hair-raising brinksmanship that causes real
uncertainty and anxiety in, you know, market

00:10:49.170 --> 00:10:52.920
participants and normal, you know, consumers.

00:10:52.920 --> 00:10:58.550
I mean, when you -- if you went outside of
Washington in October, it didn't take very

00:10:58.550 --> 00:11:03.879
much to get people to be worried about what
was happening and what was going to happen.

00:11:03.879 --> 00:11:08.059
You know, people worry about what happens
if payments aren't made, what happens if there's

00:11:08.059 --> 00:11:15.879
an economic fallout that undermines their
stability, their employment.

00:11:15.879 --> 00:11:19.499
These are not things that Congress should
play games with.

00:11:19.499 --> 00:11:20.690
It has to be done.

00:11:20.690 --> 00:11:21.790
The sooner, the better.

00:11:21.790 --> 00:11:27.839
You know, I think we get into a kind of Washington
parlor sport of trying to figure out the precise

00:11:27.839 --> 00:11:31.879
moment when, when is the last minute?

00:11:31.879 --> 00:11:34.129
It's a mistake to go to the last minute.

00:11:34.129 --> 00:11:37.339
The build-up to the last minute causes damage.

00:11:37.339 --> 00:11:40.190
It's a mistake to wait until the 11th hour.

00:11:40.190 --> 00:11:45.939
Congress should do this as quickly as possible
and as -- with the least drama as possible.

00:11:45.939 --> 00:11:52.129
And I will say that, in terms of how long
Congress has, you know, we -- I've communicated

00:11:52.129 --> 00:12:00.410
with Congress, saying we thought that the,
you know, ability to manage through the debt

00:12:00.410 --> 00:12:07.100
limit being hit on February 7th would get
us maybe to the end of February or early March.

00:12:07.100 --> 00:12:12.420
It's a very unpredictable time of year in
terms of cash flow, because people are filing

00:12:12.420 --> 00:12:14.119
for tax refunds.

00:12:14.119 --> 00:12:18.509
And when you start paying tax refunds, it's
a time of year when money goes out.

00:12:18.509 --> 00:12:23.860
You know, a month later, when people are paying
their taxes, money comes in.

00:12:23.860 --> 00:12:27.730
You cannot get from here to there without
extending the debt limit.

00:12:27.730 --> 00:12:32.799
And I think that if Congress is looking at
the numbers the way we are -- we have the

00:12:32.799 --> 00:12:37.499
best data -- they would see that they would
be looking more at the end of February than

00:12:37.499 --> 00:12:39.009
any time in March...

00:12:39.009 --> 00:12:40.009
(CROSSTALK)

00:12:40.009 --> 00:12:41.009
BUSSEY: Tighter timeframe than in the past.

00:12:41.009 --> 00:12:42.009
LEW: Yeah.

00:12:42.009 --> 00:12:43.749
BUSSEY: So let's talk a little bit about the
global economy, since that has such an impact

00:12:43.749 --> 00:12:46.569
on the health of the U.S. economy.

00:12:46.569 --> 00:12:49.189
You have just been recently in Europe.

00:12:49.189 --> 00:12:54.739
You did a trip to China and Japan and Asia
a couple of months ago, were in Europe before

00:12:54.739 --> 00:12:55.889
that.

00:12:55.889 --> 00:13:00.499
You've been counseling the Europeans, particularly
the Germans, to spend more domestically as

00:13:00.499 --> 00:13:03.149
opposed to revive their economies by exports.

00:13:03.149 --> 00:13:07.959
That sounds a little bit like John Snow saying
the same thing to China a few years ago, that

00:13:07.959 --> 00:13:11.420
you need to build up your domestic economy.

00:13:11.420 --> 00:13:15.699
The Germans kind of said, you know, "Leave
us alone, mind your own business," when you

00:13:15.699 --> 00:13:16.699
said that.

00:13:16.699 --> 00:13:21.629
Can you -- can you walk through for us what's
happening, starting with Europe?

00:13:21.629 --> 00:13:26.359
Is the crisis now abating to the point where
you can see growth organically?

00:13:26.359 --> 00:13:34.389
LEW: I think if you look at Europe now compared
to Europe in 2012, there's been a huge amount

00:13:34.389 --> 00:13:35.660
of progress.

00:13:35.660 --> 00:13:42.130
You know, we -- we don't have to worry from
day-to-day about -- about a half-a-dozen countries.

00:13:42.130 --> 00:13:44.930
But they're not completely out of the woods.

00:13:44.930 --> 00:13:51.679
They're looking at having turned the corner
from minus to either neutral or small positive.

00:13:51.679 --> 00:13:52.679
There's inconsistency.

00:13:52.679 --> 00:13:55.759
There's fragmentation in the financial markets.

00:13:55.759 --> 00:14:00.799
There's different -- very different rates
of potential growth and growth in different

00:14:00.799 --> 00:14:02.089
countries.

00:14:02.089 --> 00:14:11.109
Our message in Europe has been that you need
to do what you can do to get overall growth

00:14:11.109 --> 00:14:17.899
in Europe to a higher level, understanding
that not every country has an equal capacity,

00:14:17.899 --> 00:14:23.189
that there are surplus countries that have
the capacity to invest more and to create

00:14:23.189 --> 00:14:26.919
more domestic demand, and that would be good
for Europe's economy, it would be good for

00:14:26.919 --> 00:14:27.919
the global economy.

00:14:27.919 --> 00:14:33.149
I'd take a step back and just put the pieces
into some perspective, because this is how

00:14:33.149 --> 00:14:36.499
I think about the global economic recovery.

00:14:36.499 --> 00:14:41.980
You know, we're now in -- you know, in a growth
place that many in the world envy.

00:14:41.980 --> 00:14:43.649
You know, we're in the twos.

00:14:43.649 --> 00:14:46.449
People are talking about, can we get three?

00:14:46.449 --> 00:14:48.209
You know, they're trying to get to one.

00:14:48.209 --> 00:14:51.869
You know, there's a decimal point behind the
numbers they're looking at.

00:14:51.869 --> 00:14:59.410
You know, in our most optimistic projections,
we can't make up for Europe falling half a

00:14:59.410 --> 00:15:01.759
point or a point behind where it should be.

00:15:01.759 --> 00:15:05.639
We can't make up for China falling a half
a point or a point behind where it should

00:15:05.639 --> 00:15:06.639
be.

00:15:06.639 --> 00:15:10.269
So as I talk to my colleagues around the world,
I say we're going to do our part.

00:15:10.269 --> 00:15:11.910
We're going to keep our economy growing.

00:15:11.910 --> 00:15:17.429
We're going to shoot to hit growth targets
that -- that outperform people's expectations.

00:15:17.429 --> 00:15:19.620
But you have to do the same.

00:15:19.620 --> 00:15:25.779
So in Europe, that means countries that have
surplus doing some more to stimulate investment

00:15:25.779 --> 00:15:26.779
in demand.

00:15:26.779 --> 00:15:31.059
Frankly, you know, I think if you look at
the German coalition agreement and the policies

00:15:31.059 --> 00:15:33.839
they're putting in place, they are doing more.

00:15:33.839 --> 00:15:36.299
They've made a commitment to invest in infrastructure
this year.

00:15:36.299 --> 00:15:40.309
I wish we could make a commitment like that
here; it would be good for the United States,

00:15:40.309 --> 00:15:41.309
as well.

00:15:41.309 --> 00:15:47.379
You know, you look over the arc of the last
year, last February, March, April, the discussion

00:15:47.379 --> 00:15:53.470
was, should countries in Europe that have
to meet fiscal consolidation targets do it

00:15:53.470 --> 00:15:56.279
more quickly or over a longer time horizon?

00:15:56.279 --> 00:15:57.999
They've eased the time horizon.

00:15:57.999 --> 00:16:00.429
So I think we've made progress.

00:16:00.429 --> 00:16:06.119
And I think if you look at the questions on
banking reform, they've made progress.

00:16:06.119 --> 00:16:08.410
But in each of these cases, there's more to
do.

00:16:08.410 --> 00:16:14.639
So our conversations are -- you know, we do
have friendly differences of view on occasion,

00:16:14.639 --> 00:16:20.999
but I think, A, they have impact, and, B,
we are pushing for more, because more is good

00:16:20.999 --> 00:16:22.769
for them and it's good for us.

00:16:22.769 --> 00:16:26.180
You flip to China, it's pretty much the same
story.

00:16:26.180 --> 00:16:33.419
When I meet with my Chinese counterparts,
I am pretty confident that they are intent

00:16:33.419 --> 00:16:37.100
on the path of economic reform that they describe.

00:16:37.100 --> 00:16:44.540
At the same time, I am not confident about
the timeframe or which targets of opportunity

00:16:44.540 --> 00:16:47.739
will be sequenced early in the queue.

00:16:47.739 --> 00:16:56.609
And we -- we continue to bring an awful lot
of focus on the need for market-determined

00:16:56.609 --> 00:17:04.949
exchange rates, on competitive market determination
of allocation and price of capital.

00:17:04.949 --> 00:17:09.519
And I think that they are going to move in
that direction, and our interventions will

00:17:09.519 --> 00:17:11.959
be designed to speed that process up.

00:17:11.959 --> 00:17:14.120
Again, it's good for China's economy.

00:17:14.120 --> 00:17:18.240
I don't think we are asking either Europe
or China to do things that aren't in their

00:17:18.240 --> 00:17:19.240
own interest.

00:17:19.240 --> 00:17:24.329
BUSSEY: But let's come back to China a second
and stay on Europe just for a minute longer.

00:17:24.329 --> 00:17:31.389
One of the areas that still needs to be resolved
is financial regulatory reform.

00:17:31.389 --> 00:17:34.240
We're five years now after -- after the crisis.

00:17:34.240 --> 00:17:37.150
And still there's fundamental disagreement
within Europe, and between Europe and the

00:17:37.150 --> 00:17:45.909
United States, over banking requirements,
the levels of reserves, even executive pay,

00:17:45.909 --> 00:17:50.200
disagreements between Britain and the rest
of Europe, and Europe with the U.S.

00:17:50.200 --> 00:17:53.259
Why can't this be resolved?

00:17:53.259 --> 00:17:58.279
We are five years into the -- you know, after
the crisis.

00:17:58.279 --> 00:18:06.340
Europe needs to have that confidence in its
banking system for its economic well-being.

00:18:06.340 --> 00:18:11.929
Is this financial protectionism, financial
regulatory protectionism, in a kind of way,

00:18:11.929 --> 00:18:15.690
playing out, we don't want to restrict our
banks if you're not going to do the same thing?

00:18:15.690 --> 00:18:18.370
LEW: You know, this is very difficult stuff.

00:18:18.370 --> 00:18:21.460
And I think we in the United States have made
enormous progress.

00:18:21.460 --> 00:18:25.600
Even in this last year, we've made enormous
progress implementing financial reform.

00:18:25.600 --> 00:18:29.259
I think internationally we have made and are
continuing to make progress.

00:18:29.259 --> 00:18:34.919
I know my objective in the G-20 this year
is going to be to bring some additional focus

00:18:34.919 --> 00:18:36.289
to the international...

00:18:36.289 --> 00:18:37.289
BUSSEY: You're meeting next month, in February...

00:18:37.289 --> 00:18:38.289
(CROSSTALK)

00:18:38.289 --> 00:18:40.840
LEW: Yeah, the first meting is -- is next
-- in February in Australia.

00:18:40.840 --> 00:18:47.180
But, you know, the G-20 and the financial
stabilization board are the places where the

00:18:47.180 --> 00:18:54.149
international community comes together to
have conversations about harmonizing standards.

00:18:54.149 --> 00:19:00.690
You know, just this last weekend, some significant
progress was made in terms of international

00:19:00.690 --> 00:19:03.690
standards on capital and leverage.

00:19:03.690 --> 00:19:10.039
You know, for the last year, there's been
an argument about whether or not our differences

00:19:10.039 --> 00:19:15.019
were as big as people said or not as big,
because of differences in the way we calculate

00:19:15.019 --> 00:19:16.019
it.

00:19:16.019 --> 00:19:21.390
And I must say, as somebody who likes to be
able to compare apples to apples, when you

00:19:21.390 --> 00:19:27.100
have conversations where people are talking
past each other because, you know, one is

00:19:27.100 --> 00:19:30.799
looking at a set of numbers on a net basis
and the other is looking at them on a gross

00:19:30.799 --> 00:19:36.180
basis, and trying to do the conversion in
your head is almost impossible, there was

00:19:36.180 --> 00:19:40.250
an agreement to do it on a standardized basis
following the rule that our regulators put

00:19:40.250 --> 00:19:43.520
in place in draft rules earlier in the year.

00:19:43.520 --> 00:19:48.190
I think that opens the path for the United
States to finalize rules in a way that's consistent

00:19:48.190 --> 00:19:50.990
with international standards.

00:19:50.990 --> 00:19:58.909
And I made clear that, as we engage in conversations
internationally, we cannot weaken standards

00:19:58.909 --> 00:20:00.809
to get to international harmonization.

00:20:00.809 --> 00:20:04.790
This has to be a race to the top, not a race
to the bottom.

00:20:04.790 --> 00:20:09.980
But we should try to harmonize them to eliminate
confusion, especially where the confusion

00:20:09.980 --> 00:20:14.360
is related to technical differences, as opposed
to policy differences.

00:20:14.360 --> 00:20:21.730
You know, when I look at the challenges ahead,
I also look at what we've accomplished.

00:20:21.730 --> 00:20:27.759
That's much more capital in the United States
banks and in global banks.

00:20:27.759 --> 00:20:33.500
We've put in place a resolution program that
is something that the world is now seeing

00:20:33.500 --> 00:20:39.029
as a model, where we do stress tests, we have
living wills, we have a single point of entry

00:20:39.029 --> 00:20:41.590
in the event of a failure.

00:20:41.590 --> 00:20:46.520
And we're seeing those become things that
the world -- you know, other regulators around

00:20:46.520 --> 00:20:49.750
the world are trying to put in place.

00:20:49.750 --> 00:20:54.120
We have one country, you know, so we pass
rules, we have multiple regulators, and it's

00:20:54.120 --> 00:20:58.559
sometimes a challenge to get our regulators
to the same place, but we've been doing, you

00:20:58.559 --> 00:21:03.960
know, a lot of work to get our regulators
to consistently put forth standards in these

00:21:03.960 --> 00:21:04.960
areas.

00:21:04.960 --> 00:21:10.340
You go to Europe, and you have, you know,
multiple countries and multiple legislatures

00:21:10.340 --> 00:21:15.380
that have to take action after there's an
agreement in the larger group.

00:21:15.380 --> 00:21:19.160
So we're very sympathetic to the challenges
of coming up with policies.

00:21:19.160 --> 00:21:24.950
You know, at the end of the year, the progress
that was made in Europe on banking reform

00:21:24.950 --> 00:21:29.019
put in place a mechanism that is new and important.

00:21:29.019 --> 00:21:34.070
I mean, it is a path towards over a period
of years having a fund that banks contribute

00:21:34.070 --> 00:21:37.480
to that's loosely modeled on our FDIC.

00:21:37.480 --> 00:21:41.200
We don't think it's big enough; we don't think
it's fast enough.

00:21:41.200 --> 00:21:47.000
As I look to the future, the challenges of
2014 and beyond have to deal not with what

00:21:47.000 --> 00:21:53.429
were the risks in 2008, but what are the risks
in 2014 and beyond?

00:21:53.429 --> 00:21:56.190
And we're focusing heavily on issues like
shadow banking.

00:21:56.190 --> 00:21:58.481
Those are issues the global financial community...

00:21:58.481 --> 00:21:59.702
BUSSEY: Banking that happens outside of official
banking.

00:21:59.702 --> 00:22:02.660
LEW: Banking outside of the -- the regulated
banking system.

00:22:02.660 --> 00:22:03.660
You know...

00:22:03.660 --> 00:22:04.760
BUSSEY: Right, right.

00:22:04.760 --> 00:22:06.730
Money sloshing around that's kind of not controllable.

00:22:06.730 --> 00:22:13.940
LEW: Yeah, it -- there's a natural shift of
money from the more controlled environment

00:22:13.940 --> 00:22:18.570
to the less controlled environment, and the
risks are not always as visible because of

00:22:18.570 --> 00:22:25.390
a lighter regulatory presence and because
of a lower set of requirements.

00:22:25.390 --> 00:22:27.350
So that's an area that we're focusing on.

00:22:27.350 --> 00:22:30.780
I think my international colleagues also are
focusing on it.

00:22:30.780 --> 00:22:33.429
And I think we can make -- we have made progress.

00:22:33.429 --> 00:22:34.830
We could make more progress.

00:22:34.830 --> 00:22:38.809
Cross-border resolution, this gets to your
question about the differences of standards.

00:22:38.809 --> 00:22:44.549
You know, I think we learned from the collapse
of Lehman that, you know, the problems when

00:22:44.549 --> 00:22:50.409
a major financial institution fails are not
-- don't respect international boundaries.

00:22:50.409 --> 00:22:56.549
The transactions don't live within one country;
the implications don't live within one country.

00:22:56.549 --> 00:23:01.850
It's very important that there be confidence
that when an institution fails, if an institution

00:23:01.850 --> 00:23:06.710
fails, that the resolution internationally
works, because that's the way you can avoid

00:23:06.710 --> 00:23:12.429
the domino effect that -- that is an accelerant
to a financial and economic crisis.

00:23:12.429 --> 00:23:14.150
I think we have more work to do there.

00:23:14.150 --> 00:23:17.139
We've made progress; we need to make more
progress.

00:23:17.139 --> 00:23:21.910
BUSSEY: Just on China for a moment, before
we get to trade, and then I want to get to

00:23:21.910 --> 00:23:23.330
questions in just a second.

00:23:23.330 --> 00:23:26.760
You mentioned China's economic reform program.

00:23:26.760 --> 00:23:34.139
So far, we've heard a fair amount of discussion
of this, and it's going to focus heavily on

00:23:34.139 --> 00:23:36.740
developing out the private sector of China.

00:23:36.740 --> 00:23:39.169
Xi Jinping has said as much.

00:23:39.169 --> 00:23:42.919
Is that good or bad for American business?

00:23:42.919 --> 00:23:49.499
Is that something that's going to be more
inclusive of U.S. business or are we seeing

00:23:49.499 --> 00:23:57.870
the cultivation of national champions in -- in
China to the continued exclusion -- it's not

00:23:57.870 --> 00:24:03.019
entirely -- but to the continued exclusion
of a lot of involvement from the U.S.?

00:24:03.019 --> 00:24:08.279
This anti-monopoly law, using the anti-monopoly
law right now to hammer foreign businesses

00:24:08.279 --> 00:24:12.600
is reminiscent of the indigenous innovation
problems that American businesses have.

00:24:12.600 --> 00:24:16.100
So it doesn't feel like the situation's changing
there much.

00:24:16.100 --> 00:24:25.509
LEW: Well, the goal has to be to open markets
and to have market forces determine the answer

00:24:25.509 --> 00:24:27.950
to the question that you're asking.

00:24:27.950 --> 00:24:33.020
If there are artificial barriers in place,
then it's not really letting market forces

00:24:33.020 --> 00:24:34.360
work.

00:24:34.360 --> 00:24:37.249
I think we will get some tests pretty soon.

00:24:37.249 --> 00:24:43.990
You know, when we had our Strategic and Economic
Dialogue last summer, we had a set of important

00:24:43.990 --> 00:24:45.909
agreements come out of it.

00:24:45.909 --> 00:24:53.149
I was very pleased that China moved on opening
a Shanghai free trade zone.

00:24:53.149 --> 00:24:59.370
They moved on altering a decades-long policy
of presuming industries were closed to foreign

00:24:59.370 --> 00:25:04.249
activity to presuming that they're open unless
individually closed.

00:25:04.249 --> 00:25:07.419
We've yet to see what the implementation of
those policies look like.

00:25:07.419 --> 00:25:12.559
So if the Shanghai free trade zone opens up
to real competition, for example, to U.S.

00:25:12.559 --> 00:25:16.009
financial firms, that will be a meaningful
signal.

00:25:16.009 --> 00:25:20.679
As they go through the designations of what's
open and what's closed, if you designate everything

00:25:20.679 --> 00:25:23.919
as closed, it doesn't amount to real change.

00:25:23.919 --> 00:25:25.710
It's still early.

00:25:25.710 --> 00:25:31.660
The direction of the policies that they announced
in the third plenum was consistent with what

00:25:31.660 --> 00:25:35.840
they agreed to in our bilateral discussions.

00:25:35.840 --> 00:25:43.249
I think that they know that the -- it's critical
to the Chinese economy to let market forces

00:25:43.249 --> 00:25:48.780
drive much more, if not all, major economic
policy.

00:25:48.780 --> 00:25:54.830
They look at state-owned enterprises, and
they see built-up inventory that has nowhere

00:25:54.830 --> 00:25:55.860
to go.

00:25:55.860 --> 00:25:59.019
They see empty buildings.

00:25:59.019 --> 00:26:04.120
They know that that is not a way to maintain
the growth that they need for China to be

00:26:04.120 --> 00:26:07.309
where it needs to be in 2014, '15 and beyond.

00:26:07.309 --> 00:26:12.499
Now, at the same time, transition is difficult
in any economy.

00:26:12.499 --> 00:26:15.460
It's difficult in our economy; it's difficult
in their economy.

00:26:15.460 --> 00:26:20.240
So they are obviously concerned about the
pace at which change happens and managing

00:26:20.240 --> 00:26:21.540
it.

00:26:21.540 --> 00:26:26.669
But trying to stop it would be very damaging
to China's economy.

00:26:26.669 --> 00:26:27.799
And I think they know that.

00:26:27.799 --> 00:26:32.320
You know, I can't tell you that at the end
of this year we'll be able to say they made

00:26:32.320 --> 00:26:34.410
the move we hoped they would make.

00:26:34.410 --> 00:26:38.010
I can tell you that we're going to engage
with them at high levels on a consistent basis

00:26:38.010 --> 00:26:42.190
to keep pressing forward, making the case,
and I believe that's welcome.

00:26:42.190 --> 00:26:48.100
When I was in China just a few weeks ago,
you know, it was important for the president

00:26:48.100 --> 00:26:50.019
to meet with us, to meet with me.

00:26:50.019 --> 00:26:57.549
You know, we had an exchange that was our
second exchange in the time I've been secretary.

00:26:57.549 --> 00:27:02.809
And when I meet with my counterpart, the vice
premier, we talk about the tough issues.

00:27:02.809 --> 00:27:05.679
And they don't tell us to stay out of their
business.

00:27:05.679 --> 00:27:09.100
They want to learn from our economic experience.

00:27:09.100 --> 00:27:15.879
I think our continuing to bring focus to these
issues is something that helps them to make

00:27:15.879 --> 00:27:16.879
progress.

00:27:16.879 --> 00:27:20.750
But I approach it in a very clearheaded way.

00:27:20.750 --> 00:27:24.600
Intentions are critical, and they're important,
that it's results in the end that matter.

00:27:24.600 --> 00:27:28.730
BUSSEY: Well, one of the pressures that might
be brought to bear on China and Europe are

00:27:28.730 --> 00:27:36.080
these two new trade agreements that are being
negotiated now, the TTIP in Asia -- Trans-Pacific

00:27:36.080 --> 00:27:41.570
Partnership in Asia, and TTIP in Europe.

00:27:41.570 --> 00:27:48.279
Can you get those trade agreements without
fast-track authority from Congress?

00:27:48.279 --> 00:27:50.590
These countries don't want to be negotiating
with congresspeople.

00:27:50.590 --> 00:27:54.869
They want to be negotiating with an administration
that can promise them that the deal that they're

00:27:54.869 --> 00:27:57.650
striking is actually the deal that's going
to be put into force.

00:27:57.650 --> 00:28:02.799
LEW: Well, I think we've made it clear that
we think fast-track authority is important,

00:28:02.799 --> 00:28:05.269
and it's something we are going to work with
the Congress on.

00:28:05.269 --> 00:28:11.270
As far as the negotiations go, I remember
when we started working on the Trans-Pacific

00:28:11.270 --> 00:28:15.510
Partnership, TPP, back in 2009.

00:28:15.510 --> 00:28:21.390
And there were skeptics who said, you can't
start a conversation setting high standards

00:28:21.390 --> 00:28:24.690
with some of the smaller countries in the
region and think that the big countries are

00:28:24.690 --> 00:28:26.159
going to join.

00:28:26.159 --> 00:28:31.470
You look at where we are now, the big countries
are at the table, and even bigger countries

00:28:31.470 --> 00:28:33.670
are contemplating coming to the table.

00:28:33.670 --> 00:28:34.670
BUSSEY: Japan, yeah.

00:28:34.670 --> 00:28:39.259
LEW: Japan is at the table, and China has,
you know, mused out loud about, should they

00:28:39.259 --> 00:28:40.909
come to the table?

00:28:40.909 --> 00:28:44.009
That is an enormous change from 2009.

00:28:44.009 --> 00:28:49.730
so I think the principle of setting high-quality
standards as being the terms of the future

00:28:49.730 --> 00:28:55.809
of trade relations in the Pacific and the
Atlantic has already moved very far.

00:28:55.809 --> 00:28:58.610
There are still tough issues to be negotiated.

00:28:58.610 --> 00:29:00.860
You know, I've never been a trade negotiator.

00:29:00.860 --> 00:29:05.499
I would defer to people who've been successful
trade negotiators in the past.

00:29:05.499 --> 00:29:08.700
The tough issues are always resolved at the
end.

00:29:08.700 --> 00:29:14.850
It's not as if any party is going to, before
the final round of negotiations, make the

00:29:14.850 --> 00:29:17.659
things that are really difficult for them
at home.

00:29:17.659 --> 00:29:21.789
So we've got to keep driving forward, getting
to those final tough issues.

00:29:21.789 --> 00:29:25.429
And we have to lock in both trade agreements
and fast-track authority.

00:29:25.429 --> 00:29:27.330
We need to do both.

00:29:27.330 --> 00:29:29.850
BUSSEY: Let's -- let's get to some questions.

00:29:29.850 --> 00:29:35.200
And if I could just ask you to identify yourself
and who you're with.

00:29:35.200 --> 00:29:37.080
Yes, please?

00:29:37.080 --> 00:29:40.549
QUESTION: Hi, Nelson Cunningham with McLarty
Associates.

00:29:40.549 --> 00:29:42.170
Mr. Secretary, good to see you.

00:29:42.170 --> 00:29:44.009
Thank you for doing this.

00:29:44.009 --> 00:29:48.919
Last year, in the president's budget, for
the first time that I can recall, a Democratic

00:29:48.919 --> 00:29:55.019
president began to propose changes in the
growth of entitlement programs, designed to

00:29:55.019 --> 00:29:57.190
rein in that growth.

00:29:57.190 --> 00:30:02.840
Did it disappoint you that that conversation
was not picked up by the other side?

00:30:02.840 --> 00:30:08.049
And would you expect the president's budget
this year to re-propose and try to start that

00:30:08.049 --> 00:30:09.820
conversation once again?

00:30:09.820 --> 00:30:16.259
LEW: Look, I think we ended the year and begin
this year in a place where we've made some

00:30:16.259 --> 00:30:24.519
small progress, but important progress, getting
back to a more normal way of Congress doing

00:30:24.519 --> 00:30:27.539
fiscal policy, making budgets.

00:30:27.539 --> 00:30:29.580
And I think that's very important.

00:30:29.580 --> 00:30:34.159
Obviously, we started a year in a place where
people were talking about a grand bargain.

00:30:34.159 --> 00:30:39.710
The president put out a budget that did have
very tough policies in it that are hard in

00:30:39.710 --> 00:30:43.299
terms of making changes to entitlement programs.

00:30:43.299 --> 00:30:47.889
He also had a tax reform proposal that would've
raise revenues, so in a balanced way, we would've

00:30:47.889 --> 00:30:53.389
been able to put in place what was really
at the core of earlier negotiations that he

00:30:53.389 --> 00:30:57.700
had been having with Republican leaders.

00:30:57.700 --> 00:31:03.070
I -- having worked on these issues for most
of the last 30 years -- would have hoped that

00:31:03.070 --> 00:31:07.421
we could have reached an agreement, because
I thought then -- and I continue to think

00:31:07.421 --> 00:31:12.749
-- that would be in the best interest of our
long-term fiscal path, our long-term economic

00:31:12.749 --> 00:31:14.830
path.

00:31:14.830 --> 00:31:19.059
I think that, you know, the president's budget
will speak for itself in just a few weeks.

00:31:19.059 --> 00:31:24.239
I'm not going to get ahead of him announcing
any of his specific policies.

00:31:24.239 --> 00:31:29.730
But I think he demonstrated last year in his
budget that he was -- remained committed to

00:31:29.730 --> 00:31:36.789
a fair and balanced approach to having long-term
fiscal policies that keep us on the right

00:31:36.789 --> 00:31:38.080
path.

00:31:38.080 --> 00:31:46.070
And I think that, you know, last year's budget
reflected a deep -- a deep set of convictions.

00:31:46.070 --> 00:31:50.019
BUSSEY: Yes, please, in the back here.

00:31:50.019 --> 00:31:56.190
QUESTION: Secretary, David Sanger from the
New York Times.

00:31:56.190 --> 00:31:57.309
Good to see you.

00:31:57.309 --> 00:32:03.549
I wanted to ask you about China and cyber,
a big issue when the Sunnylands summit came

00:32:03.549 --> 00:32:08.389
up and a continuing issue at your economic
and strategic dialogue.

00:32:08.389 --> 00:32:13.080
There's a sense that the Snowden revelations
have sort of taken the pressure off of the

00:32:13.080 --> 00:32:14.080
Chinese.

00:32:14.080 --> 00:32:18.299
It has given them a way to argue -- whether
you believe the argument or not -- that we're

00:32:18.299 --> 00:32:24.480
doing something akin to what they're doing
and there's very little evidence that you

00:32:24.480 --> 00:32:29.869
see much of an abatement of the kind of behavior
that you were so concerned about, about this

00:32:29.869 --> 00:32:30.869
time last year.

00:32:30.869 --> 00:32:34.370
So I was wondering if you could give us a
sense of where that is going?

00:32:34.370 --> 00:32:39.240
LEW: So, David, I have to push back on the
premise a bit.

00:32:39.240 --> 00:32:45.809
I don't think there's any way to compare the
kinds of intelligence activities that almost

00:32:45.809 --> 00:32:54.629
every country engages in for national security
purposes with the deliberate theft of trade

00:32:54.629 --> 00:32:58.100
secrets for commercial advantage.

00:32:58.100 --> 00:32:59.980
There's no comparison.

00:32:59.980 --> 00:33:05.649
I don't think that anyone credibly believes
that there is a comparison, and I don't think

00:33:05.649 --> 00:33:10.369
that the issue has in any way been taken off
the table in terms of our conversation with

00:33:10.369 --> 00:33:11.369
the Chinese.

00:33:11.369 --> 00:33:12.970
QUESTION: (OFF-MIKE)

00:33:12.970 --> 00:33:16.840
LEW: You know, we have been very clear.

00:33:16.840 --> 00:33:23.429
I can't -- I can't tell you that -- that they
have done an about-face.

00:33:23.429 --> 00:33:30.019
But I also think they understand that for
them to play the role that they want to play

00:33:30.019 --> 00:33:35.980
in the world, as one of the great powers,
as one of the rule-makers for the world, you

00:33:35.980 --> 00:33:39.110
can't be a rule-maker and engage in those
kinds of practices.

00:33:39.110 --> 00:33:42.289
BUSSEY: Yes, in the back here.

00:33:42.289 --> 00:33:48.159
QUESTION: Barry Wood, RTHK in Hong Kong.

00:33:48.159 --> 00:33:54.740
Mr. Secretary, what about the IMF, money for
the IMF, the overdue payments that all of

00:33:54.740 --> 00:33:56.549
the other countries talk about?

00:33:56.549 --> 00:34:00.309
And what about boosting the representation
of developing countries, emerging markets

00:34:00.309 --> 00:34:01.799
within the IMF?

00:34:01.799 --> 00:34:03.360
It's moved at a snail's pace.

00:34:03.360 --> 00:34:04.869
What's your view on that?

00:34:04.869 --> 00:34:11.960
LEW: I think that the United States' commitment
to the IMF remains solid.

00:34:11.960 --> 00:34:16.319
We're the biggest contributor and participant
in the IMF.

00:34:16.319 --> 00:34:21.441
It's critically important to U.S. economic
well-being that the IMF be strong and that

00:34:21.441 --> 00:34:26.730
we have the kind of role in it that we've
had since its inception after World War II.

00:34:26.730 --> 00:34:35.149
In 2010, there was a negotiation which I think
made very important progress in terms of rebalancing

00:34:35.149 --> 00:34:43.619
some of the shares of interest in the IMF,
while maintaining the U.S. position with a

00:34:43.619 --> 00:34:49.540
significant enough share to have a controlling
voice on important questions.

00:34:49.540 --> 00:34:54.859
I have made no secret of the fact that I think
it's critical for us to finalize the ratification

00:34:54.859 --> 00:34:55.859
of those.

00:34:55.859 --> 00:35:01.660
We made a full-court press to get it done
and got close, but didn't get it done this

00:35:01.660 --> 00:35:02.800
past week.

00:35:02.800 --> 00:35:07.849
We are continuing to stand by our commitment,
and we will get it done.

00:35:07.849 --> 00:35:12.859
BUSSEY: Yes, please, right here.

00:35:12.859 --> 00:35:14.859
QUESTION: Thanks.

00:35:14.859 --> 00:35:17.280
Elisa Massimino with Human Rights First.

00:35:17.280 --> 00:35:23.109
My question is about the role of money in
the supply chains that enable mass atrocities,

00:35:23.109 --> 00:35:25.470
like those that are going on in Syria.

00:35:25.470 --> 00:35:30.160
Some people have argued that -- including
some former Treasury officials -- that the

00:35:30.160 --> 00:35:35.000
Treasury ought to take stronger action against
Russian banks that are involved directly in

00:35:35.000 --> 00:35:37.760
financing arms shipments to Assad.

00:35:37.760 --> 00:35:43.510
I wonder what you think about that and if
-- I know it can be awkward, since we're now

00:35:43.510 --> 00:35:47.480
partners with Russia in trying to resolve
the situation, but do you think Treasury ought

00:35:47.480 --> 00:35:52.609
to be taking stronger action in investigating
and even designating Russian banks that are

00:35:52.609 --> 00:35:55.680
directly involved in supporting Assad?

00:35:55.680 --> 00:36:05.420
LEW: We -- you know, through our responsibilities
in overseeing the flows of financial funds

00:36:05.420 --> 00:36:10.580
and whether or not there is compliance with
U.S. and international laws with regard to

00:36:10.580 --> 00:36:13.650
them, we have been very aggressive following
the flow of funds.

00:36:13.650 --> 00:36:19.369
And when things violate the law, we take action
without regard to the home country of the

00:36:19.369 --> 00:36:20.780
financial institution.

00:36:20.780 --> 00:36:23.380
It doesn't always make us popular.

00:36:23.380 --> 00:36:28.000
Sometimes it's friends who are very close;
sometimes it's countries with whom we have

00:36:28.000 --> 00:36:29.010
more tension.

00:36:29.010 --> 00:36:37.150
But, you know, our policy, whether it's sanctions
regarding Syria or Iran or other designated

00:36:37.150 --> 00:36:42.099
countries, the designations aren't all the
same, but our policy is to be vigilant in

00:36:42.099 --> 00:36:43.869
using these financial tools.

00:36:43.869 --> 00:36:51.119
So I would just say that we implement these
laws and we don't look at the country of origin.

00:36:51.119 --> 00:36:53.290
BUSSEY: Yes, please?

00:36:53.290 --> 00:36:56.890
QUESTION: Father Andrew from the St. Paul's
Foundation.

00:36:56.890 --> 00:37:00.910
Thank you so much for your government service,
Mr. Secretary, particularly for Tip O'Neill.

00:37:00.910 --> 00:37:03.740
I grew up in the district.

00:37:03.740 --> 00:37:09.680
St. Paul's distributes alms to the poor all
over the world, in 30 different countries,

00:37:09.680 --> 00:37:13.150
in areas of severe conflict.

00:37:13.150 --> 00:37:15.000
I want to touch back on OFAC.

00:37:15.000 --> 00:37:20.990
There seems to be an extreme level -- or extreme
lack of accountability among the federal officers

00:37:20.990 --> 00:37:22.280
at OFAC.

00:37:22.280 --> 00:37:27.200
We can't get a name, we can't get a number
when we're trying to provide food, shelter,

00:37:27.200 --> 00:37:30.160
clothing to people in refugee camps outside
of Syria.

00:37:30.160 --> 00:37:31.720
We're trying to make financial transfers.

00:37:31.720 --> 00:37:34.910
Our banks have a question for OFAC, we have
a conversation with OFAC.

00:37:34.910 --> 00:37:37.369
We can't seem to get anybody at the table.

00:37:37.369 --> 00:37:42.380
How would you propose building in accountability
so that we can provide these needed resources

00:37:42.380 --> 00:37:47.410
to people who have the opportunity to be fanatics
and fundamentalists, but aren't, precisely

00:37:47.410 --> 00:37:49.819
because of the alms and the aid that we're
giving them?

00:37:49.819 --> 00:37:54.640
LEW: You know, I think OFAC does an outstanding
job in very difficult circumstances.

00:37:54.640 --> 00:38:01.890
And there are often cases that are just heart-wrenching
in terms of flows of funds that are inconsistent

00:38:01.890 --> 00:38:08.180
with our laws, but that have the effect of
providing relief at the same time.

00:38:08.180 --> 00:38:12.700
Not just in Syria, it comes up in many troubled
parts of the world.

00:38:12.700 --> 00:38:17.400
I'm happy to look into the question of access
and communication.

00:38:17.400 --> 00:38:22.099
Obviously, we strive to a high level of clarity
and communication.

00:38:22.099 --> 00:38:28.130
But sometimes these issues relate to specific
matters that are not public matters.

00:38:28.130 --> 00:38:32.570
And I would have to go and look at the specific
matter in order to respond.

00:38:32.570 --> 00:38:35.220
QUESTION: There is a General License 11A.

00:38:35.220 --> 00:38:36.359
BUSSEY: Yes, please.

00:38:36.359 --> 00:38:37.359
Back here.

00:38:37.359 --> 00:38:38.359
Yes.

00:38:38.359 --> 00:38:41.270
QUESTION: Thank you.

00:38:41.270 --> 00:38:43.230
Good morning.

00:38:43.230 --> 00:38:46.599
Paula Stern, Stern Group.

00:38:46.599 --> 00:38:50.540
My question's about the trade promotion authority.

00:38:50.540 --> 00:38:58.220
And the headline yesterday in one of the papers
about the pushback from the Democrats.

00:38:58.220 --> 00:39:07.720
We will need bipartisan approval of authority
for fast-track.

00:39:07.720 --> 00:39:19.930
And given the fact that we started the year
with this new budget and deal in Congress,

00:39:19.930 --> 00:39:25.069
which suggests that maybe we're not going
to be as toxic this year as -- as the past,

00:39:25.069 --> 00:39:33.730
I'm wondering what your plans are, really,
for whipping into place a bipartisan authority

00:39:33.730 --> 00:39:37.010
for the president, given the pushback from
some of the Democrats?

00:39:37.010 --> 00:39:43.750
LEW: I don't think there's anything new or
unusual about the current need for there to

00:39:43.750 --> 00:39:47.650
be bipartisan support for trade promotion
authority in order for it to pass.

00:39:47.650 --> 00:39:51.089
That's always required a bipartisan effort.

00:39:51.089 --> 00:39:54.270
There's always been challenges on both sides
for different reasons.

00:39:54.270 --> 00:40:00.770
You know, we have worked on a bipartisan basis
with the leaders of the committees, and we'll

00:40:00.770 --> 00:40:04.370
continue to work on a bipartisan basis with
the Congress to get it done.

00:40:04.370 --> 00:40:07.340
The president has made clear that it's important.

00:40:07.340 --> 00:40:14.420
It's one of those issues where there is the
ability for there to be a bipartisan conversation.

00:40:14.420 --> 00:40:19.010
And it's never easy, so it's going to require
effort.

00:40:19.010 --> 00:40:24.789
BUSSEY: Do you care to venture an estimate
-- the question about a timeframe on this

00:40:24.789 --> 00:40:25.789
one?

00:40:25.789 --> 00:40:26.789
(CROSSTALK)

00:40:26.789 --> 00:40:27.880
BUSSEY: And on resolution of TPP and TTIP?

00:40:27.880 --> 00:40:35.180
LEW: I, obviously, am very hopeful that the
next round of discussions on TPP really accelerates

00:40:35.180 --> 00:40:41.100
the pace of progress, and that will have implications
in terms of other schedules.

00:40:41.100 --> 00:40:42.670
So we'll have to see how the pace goes.

00:40:42.670 --> 00:40:43.670
BUSSEY: Six months, 12 months?

00:40:43.670 --> 00:40:45.599
LEW: I'm not going to put a number on it.

00:40:45.599 --> 00:40:49.030
Obviously, Ambassador Froman has been pushing.

00:40:49.030 --> 00:40:52.320
He pushed to have the meeting at the end of
last year be an issue where the final issues

00:40:52.320 --> 00:40:53.600
get raised.

00:40:53.600 --> 00:40:59.589
He's going to continue pressing so that those
issues, those hard issues that require countries

00:40:59.589 --> 00:41:03.760
to kind of move across the finish line get
queued up.

00:41:03.760 --> 00:41:05.170
And I believe it will happen.

00:41:05.170 --> 00:41:07.440
BUSSEY: Ambassador Froman, the U.S. trade
representative...

00:41:07.440 --> 00:41:09.770
LEW: U.S. trade representative.

00:41:09.770 --> 00:41:12.670
BUSSEY: Yes, please, right here.

00:41:12.670 --> 00:41:18.240
QUESTION: Twenty years ago, Jim Baker engineered
the Plaza Agreement.

00:41:18.240 --> 00:41:22.299
And with the devaluation of the dollar, it
stabilized our current accounts for almost

00:41:22.299 --> 00:41:23.299
10 years.

00:41:23.299 --> 00:41:27.900
Recently, the Japanese have engineered a 20
percent devaluation of their currency.

00:41:27.900 --> 00:41:31.710
Would you expect it to have a similar impact
on Japan's trade account?

00:41:31.710 --> 00:41:35.640
And how will the South Koreans and others
react to this new change in their competitiveness?

00:41:35.640 --> 00:41:42.210
LEW: You know, I think the challenges facing
Japan and their economy have been profound

00:41:42.210 --> 00:41:44.440
for almost two decades.

00:41:44.440 --> 00:41:51.349
I think, you know, it has been a recurring
argument made by the United States, the international

00:41:51.349 --> 00:41:58.890
community that Japan needed to take action
to reverse almost two decades of deflation

00:41:58.890 --> 00:42:00.530
and economic decline.

00:42:00.530 --> 00:42:03.789
You know, they have put in place policies.

00:42:03.789 --> 00:42:09.900
We still have to see what the so-called third
arrow of their policies are.

00:42:09.900 --> 00:42:15.230
Those are the real reforms that will determine
whether or not the future is one where they'll

00:42:15.230 --> 00:42:21.450
grow their domestic economy, grow domestic
demand, and be able to continue to have the

00:42:21.450 --> 00:42:25.440
kind of growth that they're now seeing because
of the shorter-term policies they've put into

00:42:25.440 --> 00:42:27.099
effect.

00:42:27.099 --> 00:42:32.260
I think it was very important that last year,
roughly this time, in February, there was

00:42:32.260 --> 00:42:39.180
an agreement at the G-7 and the G-20 that
has stuck, which is that countries will be

00:42:39.180 --> 00:42:45.230
-- hold themselves to the agreed-upon rule
that they will use domestic tools for domestic

00:42:45.230 --> 00:42:47.690
purposes to grow their economy.

00:42:47.690 --> 00:42:53.490
That is a critically important frame, and
it's something that Japan has to stick to.

00:42:53.490 --> 00:42:58.569
You know, they need to get their domestic
economy growing.

00:42:58.569 --> 00:43:04.400
Their long-term growth can't be rooted in
a strategy that ultimately turns in any way

00:43:04.400 --> 00:43:12.359
towards, you know, reliance on unfair advantage
because of exchange rate.

00:43:12.359 --> 00:43:14.900
You know, we have kept an eye on that.

00:43:14.900 --> 00:43:18.310
Our currency report analyzed it very closely.

00:43:18.310 --> 00:43:20.700
We continue to analyze it very closely.

00:43:20.700 --> 00:43:25.440
And we said, as long as they stick to those
rules, domestic tools for domestic purposes,

00:43:25.440 --> 00:43:28.640
it's in Japan's interests and the world's
interests for Japan's economy to grow.

00:43:28.640 --> 00:43:34.280
You know, the three big -- you know, four
big economies of the world are the United

00:43:34.280 --> 00:43:36.080
States, Europe, China and Japan.

00:43:36.080 --> 00:43:43.390
If any one of them is performing particularly
sub-par, it creates problems that flow well

00:43:43.390 --> 00:43:45.320
beyond the borders.

00:43:45.320 --> 00:43:48.790
And there's a balance where -- you know, we've
got to keep an eye on it.

00:43:48.790 --> 00:43:56.770
We can't let it turn from the kinds of policies
that are domestic policies for domestic purposes,

00:43:56.770 --> 00:43:58.760
but it is important for Japan, also, to grow.

00:43:58.760 --> 00:44:03.090
BUSSEY: So we have two minutes' time for just
one more question, right down here, the back

00:44:03.090 --> 00:44:04.920
of the room.

00:44:04.920 --> 00:44:07.970
QUESTION: Thank you very much.

00:44:07.970 --> 00:44:11.470
My name is Chi Mi Nguyen (ph) with Voice of
Vietnamese Americans.

00:44:11.470 --> 00:44:12.990
Thank you, Secretary.

00:44:12.990 --> 00:44:19.089
I come back to your focus on standards and
implementation.

00:44:19.089 --> 00:44:26.069
And I'd like to hear from you the lessons
learned from our WTO and the coming TPP and

00:44:26.069 --> 00:44:34.000
TTIPs and other free trade agreements, because
I think there's a lot of doubt and uncertainty

00:44:34.000 --> 00:44:40.710
in our (inaudible) regarding the unemployment
that we've been experiencing for the last

00:44:40.710 --> 00:44:43.160
5 years, actually for the last 10 years.

00:44:43.160 --> 00:44:49.809
You see, since the WTO, we have suffered the
currency war and many other wars with rising

00:44:49.809 --> 00:44:51.450
power China.

00:44:51.450 --> 00:44:59.339
And the recent still anti-dumping situation
with the WTO that's happened threatened our

00:44:59.339 --> 00:45:01.589
industry and our market.

00:45:01.589 --> 00:45:09.099
So our question to you is, as the treasurer,
secretary of treasury, where do you see we

00:45:09.099 --> 00:45:18.410
need to do to ensure the implementation phase
of our agreements to secure our currency and

00:45:18.410 --> 00:45:19.410
our jobs?

00:45:19.410 --> 00:45:20.670
Thank you, for the U.S., for the Americans.

00:45:20.670 --> 00:45:27.610
LEW: Yeah, I think we have been very clear
in all of our conversations, in the multilateral

00:45:27.610 --> 00:45:34.750
negotiations and our bilateral conversations,
that we need a level playing field with clear

00:45:34.750 --> 00:45:37.619
rules, and those rules need to be enforced.

00:45:37.619 --> 00:45:43.510
So taking enforcement actions to make sure
that the agreements are adhered to is a critical

00:45:43.510 --> 00:45:47.500
part of having effective rules of the road.

00:45:47.500 --> 00:45:52.170
Rules that aren't followed aren't really rules.

00:45:52.170 --> 00:45:57.110
So that's why WTO actions are brought from
time to time.

00:45:57.110 --> 00:46:02.349
And the goal is to have countries observe
the rules, not to be in a place where one

00:46:02.349 --> 00:46:10.130
has to be taking actions to -- whether it's
for one industry or another -- to enforce

00:46:10.130 --> 00:46:11.210
the rules.

00:46:11.210 --> 00:46:14.289
I think that if you go back to what I was
saying a few minutes ago, in terms of the

00:46:14.289 --> 00:46:17.829
spirit of the negotiation of TPP, it's very
encouraging.

00:46:17.829 --> 00:46:24.089
You know, I was just recently in Asia, went
to a range of countries with extremely different

00:46:24.089 --> 00:46:25.130
economies.

00:46:25.130 --> 00:46:30.150
I was in Japan, China, Malaysia, Vietnam,
and Singapore.

00:46:30.150 --> 00:46:36.630
In each of the countries, at a very different
point of internal economic development, there

00:46:36.630 --> 00:46:40.450
was a desire to participate in a high-standard
trade agreement.

00:46:40.450 --> 00:46:45.619
There was also anxiety about, what did that
mean in terms of the domestic economy?

00:46:45.619 --> 00:46:47.200
What would be the transition rules?

00:46:47.200 --> 00:46:53.400
What would be the reflection of the fact that
countries don't come in exactly the same position?

00:46:53.400 --> 00:46:59.000
And those are the kinds of issues that are
being negotiated in the TPP with a sensitivity

00:46:59.000 --> 00:47:06.289
to the fact that if we get all of the countries
to credible and enforceable, high-level rules,

00:47:06.289 --> 00:47:09.700
it's good for each of us individually and
it's good for us globally.

00:47:09.700 --> 00:47:15.500
But it's not good if the rules are broken
and we need to be vigilant as we are to make

00:47:15.500 --> 00:47:19.369
sure that, when we make agreements, the agreements
are kept.

00:47:19.369 --> 00:47:24.299
BUSSEY: Secretary Lew, you've got to get to
work before the stock market opens at 9:30,

00:47:24.299 --> 00:47:28.760
so I want to -- I want to thank you and -- for
joining us today and also thank the Council

00:47:28.760 --> 00:47:30.680
on Foreign Relations for holding this event.

00:47:30.680 --> 00:47:31.680
LEW: Thank you very much, John.

00:47:31.680 --> 00:47:32.680
BUSSEY: Thanks very much.

00:47:32.680 --> 00:47:33.680
(APPLAUSE)

00:47:33.680 --> 00:47:33.681
END

